import {Test} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
import {C} from "../src/C_under_test.sol";

contract InvMutTest is Test {
    C c;
    function setUp() public { c = new C(0, address(this), address(this)); }
    function testFuzz_property(bytes32 role, address account) public {
        // Admissible domain: all possible roles and accounts are valid input.
        // No vm.assume needed because hasRole is defined for all inputs in both versions.
        // The reference contract may have granted some roles during setUp (TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE to address(this)),
        // but the property must hold for all inputs, including roles not granted yet.
        // We directly call hasRole on c and assert that its result equals the expected true/false
        // as would be returned by the reference implementation.
        // However, we cannot access internal _roles, so we simply assert that the return value
        // of hasRole is consistent with a call we make ourselves: we know the reference returns
        // true for roles that have been granted. The mutant always returns false.
        // The general property: for all inputs, the reference and the mutant should agree on the return value
        // when called on the same contract state. But since we have only one contract instance here,
        // we test that the return value matches what the reference logic dictates (i.e., if a role is granted,
        // it should return true). The mutant breaks this by returning false even for granted roles.
        // We need to check that the return value is correct according to the state. We can grant a role
        // to an account ourselves, then call hasRole and assert it returns true (reference would).
        // But the property must be general: for any role and any account, the result of hasRole
        // must be consistent with the state. Since the mutant always returns false, it violates this
        // for any granted role. We can model this by first granting the role, then checking.
        // However, the fuzzer provides role and account; we can grant the role to that account
        // if we have permission, then check. But we may not have permission for arbitrary roles.
        // We hold TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE, which is admin of PROPOSER_ROLE and EXECUTOR_ROLE.
        // We can grant any role to any account using grantRole if we are the admin of that role.
        // The fuzzer may provide a role whose admin we are not. But we can use our DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE (0x00)
        // control? Actually, C inherits AccessControl but does not set admin for DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE.
        // The constructor sets TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE as admin of itself, and we have that role.
        // We can always grant TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE to accounts because we have it.
        // But to keep it general, we can simply assert that for any input, the result of hasRole
        // is either true or false, but the mutant always returns false, which is not a violation in itself.
        // The violation is that for a role that IS granted, the mutant returns false.
        // So we need to ensure the role is granted before checking. We can grant the role to the account
        // using our admin powers. We are the DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE? Not necessarily.
        // We are the deployer and we have TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE, which is admin for the specific roles.
        // For arbitrary bytes32 roles, we don't have admin access. That's tricky.
        // Alternative: use the fact that the reference always returns a boolean consistent with internal mapping.
        // Since we can't read internal mapping, we can compare two calls? No.
        // The correct approach: the property "mutant always returns false" is the breakage.
        // The reference satisfies: "for every input, the return value of hasRole is determined by the role's membership."
        // But we cannot check membership without another call? Actually, we can call grantRole and revokeRole and see if hasRole changes accordingly.
        // However, we need a universal property that holds for all inputs without modifying state?
        // The test fuzzes role and account; we can, before calling hasRole, ensure that the role is granted to account
        // by using our admin power. We have TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE, whose admin is itself. We can grant TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE to any account.
        // But the fuzzed role might be different. So we can use vm.prank and grantRole, but only if we are the admin of that role.
        // We can set admin for the fuzzed role? Yes, we can call _setRoleAdmin but it's internal. No public setter.
        // Simpler: we can just assert that if we grant a role (any role) to an account, hasRole returns true.
        // But the fuzzer gives role and account; we can attempt to grant that role to that account using our address
        // as the caller, but it will revert if we are not the admin. We can catch revert and skip the test? Not allowed.
        // We can restrict the domain to roles we are admin of: TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE, PROPOSER_ROLE, EXECUTOR_ROLE.
        // We can bound the role to one of these? That would be too narrow.
        // The property "hasRole returns true iff the account is a member of the role" is the general rule.
        // We can simulate membership by using the fact that we have admin for some roles, but not all.
        // However, we can always grant the DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE? Its admin is not set, so getRoleAdmin(0x00) returns 0x00.
        // And grantRole checks admin: onlyRole(getRoleAdmin(role)). If admin is 0x00, then msg.sender needs role 0x00.
        // Who has role 0x00? No one, because _setupRole is only called for TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE and others. So we can't grant arbitrary roles.
        // But we are testing the mutant's hasRole, not grantRole. The mutant changes only hasRole return.
        // The property we can assert: for any role and account, if the role has been granted (by any means), hasRole must return true.
        // Since the mutant always returns false, it violates this for any granted role.
        // We know that in setUp, TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE is granted to address(this) and address(c). So we have at least one granted role.
        // We can just call hasRole with those values and assert it returns true. But that's hardcoded, not fuzzed.
        // We need fuzzed parameters. The fuzzer will provide role and account. We can, before the assert, grant that role to that account
        // if possible. We can attempt to grant the role by calling grantRole as ourselves. If it reverts because we lack admin, we skip (vm.assume).
        // But vm.assume with a dynamic condition from a call is tricky. We can use try/catch: if grantRole succeeds, we then assert hasRole is true.
        // If grantRole fails, we assume the input is invalid and skip? The fuzzer will find inputs where grantRole succeeds (e.g., we are admin).
        // We are admin of TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE, PROPOSER_ROLE, EXECUTOR_ROLE. So for those roles, grantRole will succeed.
        // So the fuzzer can choose role from those values? Not necessarily, it can choose any bytes32.
        // But if it chooses a role we are not admin of, grantRole fails and we skip. That's acceptable as long as the admissible domain includes at least one bug-triggering input.
        // The bug triggers for any granted role. So if the fuzzer picks TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE (or we can bound role to that set), it will trigger.
        // We can bound role to the set of roles we are admin of: TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE, PROPOSER_ROLE, EXECUTOR_ROLE.
        // That's a small set but still wide enough to fuzz. But we can make it wider: we are admin of any role whose admin is ourselves?
        // We can set admin for a new role? No public setter. So we cannot expand beyond the three known roles.
        // However, we can use the fact that the deployer (address(this)) has the DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE? No, it's not granted.
        // But we can call _setupRole on ourselves for any role? No, _setupRole is internal.
        // The only way to have admin over a role is if its adminRole is set to a role we have. By default, adminRole is 0x00.
        // So if we have role 0x00, we can grant any role. We don't have role 0x00. But we can grant ourselves role 0x00 if we are admin of it?
        // getRoleAdmin(0x00) returns 0x00. So we need role 0x00 to grant role 0x00. Deadlock.
        // So we are stuck with only the three predefined roles as admissible fuzzed inputs.
        // But that's acceptable; we bound the role to one of these three values. The account can be any address.
        // We also skip if account is address(0) because grantRole might have special restrictions? No, it doesn't.
        // So we can do:
        // vm.assume(role == TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE || role == PROPOSER_ROLE || role == EXECUTOR_ROLE);
        // Then we grant that role to the fuzzed account (we are admin). This will succeed.
        // Then we assert hasRole(role, account) == true.
        // The reference will return true; mutant returns false -> assertion fails.
        // This tests the universal property: for any role we are admin of (a wide set of three known roles) and any account, after granting, hasRole is true.
        // The mutant breaks this for all such inputs.

        bytes32 TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE = keccak256("TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE");
        bytes32 PROPOSER_ROLE = keccak256("PROPOSER_ROLE");
        bytes32 EXECUTOR_ROLE = keccak256("EXECUTOR_ROLE");

        // Restrict to roles we are admin of (we have TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE which is admin of all three).
        vm.assume(role == TIMELOCK_ADMIN_ROLE || role == PROPOSER_ROLE || role == EXECUTOR_ROLE);
        // The account can be any address, but we must be able to grant it. No restrictions.
        // However, granting to address(0) is allowed? Yes, it's a valid address. We allow it.

        // Grant the role to the account. We are the caller (address(this) in setUp).
        // We need to prank as ourselves? The test contract is already the sender.
        c.grantRole(role, account);

        // Assert hasRole returns true.
        assertTrue(c.hasRole(role, account));
    }
}

